[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5  (5. Bf4  Be7 6. e3 c6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 {When White has already put the bishop on f4, do not play automatic ...h6. Finish development, use ...Nbd7, and prepare ...Re8 or ...Bf5/g6 setups depending on White's pieces.}) c6 {Both GM sources return to this as the basic Exchange structure. Finegold explains that ...c6 defends d5 and gives Black a solid base before moving the light-square bishop. Neiksans stresses that White's dream is a minority attack against c6, so Black must know why the pawn is there and not treat it as a random developing move.}  (5... Be7 {Finegold notes that early ...Be7 is a precise QGD move order if Black wants to avoid some Queen-f3 systems, but it gives White independent Exchange tries.}  6. e3 O-O 7. Bd3 c6) 6. e3 Be7  (6... h6 {Neiksans is very explicit that this early ...h6 is a smart Black move order. It asks the bishop a question before White has settled the ideal setup.}  7. Bh4 Be7 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Nf3 Ne4 {The point of early ...h6: after the bishop is on h4, Black keeps access to the important ...Ne4 idea. If White trades on e7, Black recaptures and the knight can return to f6 without structural damage.}) 7. Bd3 h6 {Your deck reaches this position often. The lesson is not just "kick the bishop"; it is to control whether White can use Bf4/Qf3 ideas against the pinned knight and your light-square bishop.} 8. Bh4 O-O 9. Nge2  (9. Nf3  Re8 10. O-O Nbd7 {Against normal development, use the calm QGD shell: ...Re8, ...Nbd7, then look for ...Nf8, ...Bd6, ...Ne4, or a prepared ...c5.}) Re8 10. O-O Nbd7 {This is a useful stopping point from the weak file. Black's next decisions are plan-based: trade the light-square bishop if White lets you, meet f3/e4 ideas with timely ...c5, and never forget that White's queenside minority attack is aimed at c6.} 11. f3 b5 12. a3 a5 13. Bf2 {[%cal Gd7b6,Gb6c4]} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - QGD Bishop Activity"]
[Site "YouTube"]
[Date "2023.10.24"]
