[Annotator "GM Ben Finegold; transcript-derived notes"]
[Source "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XOW31xd1PL0"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation"]
[Start "[0,0,0]"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Be7 6. Bg5 c6 7. e3 Bf5 {This is the clean version of the reference move in your weak deck. Finegold's practical message is simple: if Black can play ...Bf5 and then develop normally, Black has solved the main QGD problem. White often has to play sharply to challenge it. In this Nf3 move order, White no longer has the Queen-f3 punishment because the knight occupies f3.} (7... h6 8. Bh4 O-O 9. Bd3 Re8 10. Qc2 Nbd7 {If you choose the quieter route, this transposes back into the solid Exchange shell from game 1.}) 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Nbd7 10. O-O O-O 11. Rab1 Re8 {Against the minority attack, Black should not drift. Coordinate the rooks, watch b7 and d5, and be ready for ...a5 or ...c5 when White commits.} 12. b4 a6 13. a4 Bd6 {A representative regrouping move. Black eyes h2, supports possible ...Ne4, and keeps the c7/c6 complex defended before White lands b5.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - QGD Tartakower and Fianchetto"]
[Site "YouTube"]
[Date "2023.10.24"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Queen's Gambit Training"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Ben Finegold; transcript-derived notes"]
[Source "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XOW31xd1PL0"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[Start "[0,0,0]"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 (3... Be7 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 c6 7. O-O Nbd7 {This covers the g3 QGD positions in the weak file. Keep the same Orthodox attitude: ...c6 supports d5, ...Nbd7 finishes development, and Black waits for the right ...dxc4 or ...c5 break instead of throwing in weakening rook-pawn moves.}) 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 {Finegold points to the Tartakower with ...b6 as a classic Karpov-style QGD choice. The purpose is to solve the light-square bishop and keep Black structurally sound.} 8. Bd3 dxc4 {This is the move your deck asks you to study. Once White has spent time on Bd3 and Black has ...b6 ready, capturing on c4 can gain a tempo and open the b7 bishop.} 9. Bxc4 Bb7 10. O-O Nbd7 11. Qe2 a6 12. Rfd1 Ne4 {A model continuation: trade pieces when useful, keep the center under control, and remember Finegold's broader QGD lesson that Black often wins by being solid first, then using ...c5 or piece activity when White overextends.} *
